      <floats-group id="article-17768.s17">
        <fig id="article-17768.image.f1" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Scalp Defect in Aplasia Cutis Congenita. The lesion appears as a small, atrophic patch without hair, with surrounding hair radiating outward in a whorl pattern. <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://dermnetnz.org/images">DermNet New Zealand</ext-link></p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="aplasia__cutis" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-17768.s18">
        <fig id="article-17768.image.f2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Dermoscopy&#x000a0;Findings in Membranous Aplasia Cutis Congenita. The&#x000a0;top image shows a well-demarcated, translucent scalp lesion covered by a thin, shiny epithelial membrane&#x02014;classic for the membranous type of this condition. The bottom image demonstrates the absence of skin appendages and marked thinning of the epidermis within the lesion, consistent with the diagnosis. Contributed by Haitham M. Saleh, MSc</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="IMG-0706" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
